  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Alaska received $4.1B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, with 10,224 awards and an average award size of $397K. Obligations rose up 5.3% year over year, and current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is still in progress. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $1.6B in obligations, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $891M and the General Services Administration with $502M. Construction dominates industry spending, with Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $1.3B. Major vendors include DAVIS CONSTRUCTORS & ENGINEERS, INC. at $470M and TUTOR PERINI CORPORATION at $459M. Other sizable industry categories are Pharmacy Benefit Management and Other Third Party Administration of Insurance and Pension Funds at $288M, Facilities Support Services at $245M, and Engineering Services at $191M. This snapshot focuses on obligations only, and the fiscal year to date totals do not represent a full year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in spending compared with last year?",
        "body": "Alaska's obligations rose up 5.3% year over year in FY2026 to date. Total obligations reached $4.1B, up from $3.9B in FY25 and $3.8B in FY24. The increase is driven in part by higher activity in construction and defense-related buying. Award volume remains substantial at 10,224 awards with an average award size of $397K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where is federal contract money concentrated in Alaska?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense leads buying with $1.6B in obligations, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $891M and the General Services Administration at $502M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry with $1.3B in obligations. Large vendors are concentrated in construction and support services, led by DAVIS CONSTRUCTORS & ENGINEERS, INC. at $470M and TUTOR PERINI CORPORATION at $459M."
      }
    ]
  },
